title: Bybit / Safe{Wallet} Cold Wallet Theft
page: breaches/bybit-safe-wallet.html
as-of: 2026-11-23
promise: A developer's Mac led to tampered JavaScript in Safe's S3 bucket, and Bybit's signers approved a disguised delegatecall; the FBI puts the theft at about $1.5 billion.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: ~$1.5B | in virtual assets stolen from Bybit, per the FBI | Elliptic counts about $1.46 billion
stat: ~2 | days the tampered code was served before the theft | Removed about two minutes after it (Sygnia)
stat: 17 | days from the developer's Mac to the theft | February 4 to 21, by Safe's timeline
North Korea, per the FBI, which calls the activity TraderTraitor, as it does the 2026 Binance theft. Mandiant tracks UNC4899 (CryptoSlate, reporting Safe's update). ATT&CK has no TraderTraitor group entry.
notes:
Start with the figures the page leads with, and with who stands behind each one.

The FBI puts the theft at about $1.5 billion in virtual assets. Elliptic counts about $1.46 billion. Say whose figure you are quoting.

The tampered code was served for about two days before the theft, from 15:29 UTC on February 19. It was removed about two minutes after the theft transaction, per Sygnia. Keep that order when you say it aloud: two days before, two minutes after.

Seventeen days passed from the developer's Mac being compromised, on February 4, to the theft on February 21, by Safe's timeline.

The FBI says North Korea was responsible and calls the activity TraderTraitor, the same label it gives the 2026 Binance hot wallet theft, which has its own csoh.org kill chain. Mandiant tracks the group as UNC4899, per CryptoSlate's report of Safe's March 6 update. ATT&CK has no TraderTraitor group entry, so the deck names no ATT&CK group.

The route ran through Safe{Wallet}'s cloud, not Bybit's. Bybit says both forensic firms it hired found no compromise of its own infrastructure.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: February 4 | A Safe{Wallet} developer's Mac compromised through a Docker project
event: February 5 | The developer's MFA-backed AWS session used in Safe's account
event: February 18 | Two attacker contracts deployed (CertiK)
event: February 19, about 15:29 | JavaScript overwritten in Safe's S3 bucket
event: February 21, 14:13:35 | The disguised delegatecall executes; the sweep follows
event: February 21, about 14:15 | Clean file back (Sygnia), before the main sweep
Times are UTC. The code was live about two days before the theft and about two minutes after it. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
Every date on this slide is one the page gives, and the markers are evenly spaced, not to scale.

On February 4, 2025, a Safe{Wallet} developer's Mac was compromised through a Docker project that talked to a domain registered two days earlier. From February 5 the attackers used that developer's MFA-backed AWS session tokens inside Safe's AWS account. Between February 5 and 17 Safe describes reconnaissance and little else.

On February 18, per CertiK, the attackers deployed two contracts. At about 15:29 UTC on February 19 they overwrote JavaScript that app.safe.global served from Safe's S3 bucket through CloudFront. The new code fired only for Bybit's cold wallet or one other address.

At 14:13:35 UTC on February 21, Bybit's cold wallet executed a transaction its three signers had approved as a routine transfer. It was a delegatecall that handed control of the wallet to an attacker contract, and about two and a half minutes later the wallet was swept. About two minutes after the theft transaction, per Sygnia, and before the main sweep, the clean code went back.

On February 26 the FBI named North Korea, and Bybit published the preliminary reports from Sygnia and Verichains. Safe's update with Mandiant came on March 6.

== Safe's first statement was contradicted, then superseded by its own timeline
layout: table
row: When | Who | What they said
row: February 21 | Safe (crypto.news) | Had "not found evidence that the official Safe frontend was compromised" at the time
row: February 26 | Sygnia, Verichains | Malicious JavaScript in files from Safe's S3 bucket
row: February 26 | Safe | Compromised developer machine; external researchers: no flaw in contracts, frontend or services source
row: March 6 | Safe, with Mandiant | Hijacked AWS session tokens; "malicious JS code inserted"; Sygnia: "did not independently confirm" it
row: March 16 | Sygnia | Largest remaining gap: a comprehensive investigation of Safe's AWS account
notes:
Safe's statements and the investigators' findings differ, and in one place they contradict. This table puts each with its date and its source.

On February 21 Safe posted that it had "not found evidence that the official Safe frontend was compromised," as crypto.news reported. That was what Safe said it had found at the time.

Sygnia and Verichains, in preliminary reports Bybit published on February 26, both placed malicious JavaScript in files that app.safe.global served from Safe's S3 bucket. Safe's own March 6 timeline later listed a snapshot of the site "with malicious JS code inserted," so its timeline superseded the first statement.

The March 6 update, prepared with Mandiant, describes the hijacked AWS session tokens and the JavaScript change. Sygnia notes that the Mandiant material Safe shared mentions the change but "did not independently confirm" it through a forensic investigation of Safe's infrastructure.

Safe also said it had rebuilt its infrastructure and rotated all credentials, "ensuring the attack vector is fully eliminated." Sygnia wrote on March 16 that critical gaps remained, the largest being a comprehensive investigation of activity in Safe's AWS account.

Safe said on February 26 that it would publish a full post-mortem. The page found none later than the March 6 update. The February 26 statement also differs in framing; the next slide sets it out.

== Safe's February 26 statement differs in framing
layout: compare
col: Matches the investigators | None alleges a source or contract flaw | Sygnia: JavaScript modified "directly on the S3 bucket" | Elastic: the app subverted without a breach of backend APIs or contract logic
col: What it leaves out | AWS, S3 and the served JavaScript | Safe-served code built the transaction in signers' browsers (Sygnia, NCC) | The developer's machine was the way into AWS
Safe said "a compromised Safe{Wallet} developer machine" resulted in "the proposal of a disguised malicious transaction"; external researchers found no flaw in contracts or frontend source. The difference is framing: source code against a file deployed to S3.
notes:
Safe's February 26 statement said the attack came through "a compromised Safe{Wallet} developer machine" resulting in "the proposal of a disguised malicious transaction." It said external researchers found no vulnerabilities in its smart contracts or the "source code of the frontend and services."

On those terms it matches the investigators. None of them alleges a flaw in Safe's source code or contracts. Sygnia says the JavaScript was modified "directly on the S3 bucket," and Elastic describes the app being subverted without a breach of backend APIs or contract logic. The source was not changed; the deployed file was.

The difference is framing, between source code and a file deployed to S3, and what the statement leaves out. It does not mention AWS, S3 or the served JavaScript. The disguised transaction was built in Bybit's signers' browsers by code served from Safe's infrastructure, per Sygnia and NCC Group, and the developer's machine was the way into AWS.

When you present this, attribute each side. The page calls the difference one of framing, and of what the statement leaves out.

== This page's own readings of the archive and the chain
layout: table
row: Evidence | This page's reading
row: Archived bundle headers | Malicious copy Last-Modified 15:29:43 UTC, February 19; restored copy 14:15:32, February 21
row: Safe's timeline times | 15:29:25 and 14:15:13: file times, probably the second file's, not snapshots
row: Archived version IDs | Unversioned while tampered code was live; versioned now; no source states this
row: Page archived February 20 | No integrity attributes; CSP script-src allowed 'self'
row: Second targeted Safe | Nine delegatecalls into the slot-0 writer, February 18 to 20: an apparent rehearsal wallet
notes:
The page separates what its sources say from what it reads for itself, and labels its own readings as such. This slide gathers those readings so you can tell them apart from the investigators' findings.

From the Internet Archive's response headers: the malicious main bundle carries a Last-Modified time of 15:29:43 UTC on February 19, and the restored copy 14:15:32 on February 21, with the same ETag as the February 13 build. Safe's timeline gives 15:29:25 and 14:15:13 and labels them Wayback Machine snapshots, but the archive holds no capture at either time, and 14:15:13 is the Last-Modified time Sygnia shows for the second file; the page reads them as file modification times, probably the second file's.

No version ID on the malicious copy and a null one on the restored copy lead the page to read the bucket as unversioned then. It is versioned now. No source states this.

The archived app.safe.global/welcome from February 20 had no integrity attributes, and its Content Security Policy allowed scripts from its own origin. The page also measured about 2 KB injected into a file of about 3.7 MB.

On chain, a second Safe ran nine delegatecalls into the attackers' contract, and Bybit's wallet had never made a delegatecall before the theft. The page infers a rehearsal; no published source says so.

== The kill chain, stages 01 to 05
layout: flow
step: 01 | Initial Access | Docker project on a Mac | T1204.002
step: 02 | Credential Access | Live AWS session taken | T1528
step: 03 | Lateral Movement | Into Safe's AWS account | T1550.001
step: 04 | Discovery | Twelve days, mostly undisclosed | T1580
step: 05 | Resource Development | Contracts; apparent rehearsal | T1608
Arrows show the page's stage order. The rehearsal at stage 05 is this page's reading.
notes:
The page has nine stages, so the chain takes two slides. Each step shows its phase and one ATT&CK technique the page maps to it.

Stage 01 is initial access: a Docker project on a Safe{Wallet} developer's Mac, talking to a newly registered domain. Stage 02 is credential access: the developer's active, MFA-backed AWS session token, likely taken from the Mac. Stage 03 is lateral movement into Safe's AWS account with that session, on the developer's working hours, and a failed attempt to add the attackers' own MFA device. Stage 04 is twelve days of discovery in AWS that Safe has described as little more than reconnaissance. Stage 05 is resource development on chain: two attacker contracts, and what the page reads as a rehearsal on a second Safe.

The first half runs through a developer's machine, Safe's AWS account and, at stage 05, the chain itself. Bybit's own infrastructure does not appear in it.

== The kill chain, stages 06 to 09
layout: flow
step: 06 | Supply Chain | JavaScript swapped in S3 | T1195.002
step: 07 | Stealth | Two Safes; signers misled | T1480
step: 08 | Impact | Delegatecall, then the sweep | T1657
step: 09 | Stealth | Clean file back | T1070
Stage 06 is the cloud lesson: nothing is reported to have checked the files in Safe's S3 bucket.
notes:
The second half runs from Safe's S3 bucket to Bybit's cold wallet.

Stage 06 is the supply chain step: JavaScript that app.safe.global served from Safe's S3 bucket through CloudFront, overwritten under its original file name. Stage 07 is that code at work in the signers' browsers: it fired only for Bybit's cold wallet or one other Safe, swapped a plain transfer for a delegatecall, and put the original details back once the wallet had signed. Stage 08 is the impact: the delegatecall replaced the wallet's implementation and the wallet was swept. Stage 09 is the cleanup, the clean file put back about two minutes after the theft transaction and the malware removed from the Mac.

The page files stages 07 and 09 both under Stealth.

The page puts its cloud lesson at stage 06, where nothing is reported to have checked the files in the bucket. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== A Docker project on a developer's Mac
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1204.002 | User Execution: Malicious File
technique: T1583.001 | Acquire Infrastructure: Domains
fact: When | February 4, 2025, 08:55:45 UTC (Safe's timeline)
fact: Lure | a Docker project in ~/Downloads; its arrival is not published
fact: Domain | getstockprice[.]com, registered February 2 via Namecheap
fact: Tooling | Mythic, per Mandiant as relayed by Sygnia
Code of unknown origin ran on a machine with a live session into production AWS.
notes:
Safe's timeline, built with Mandiant, records the developer it calls Developer1 as compromised at 08:55:45 UTC on February 4, 2025. Safe says this developer "was one of the very few personnel that had higher access," per The Hacker News.

The earliest malicious activity Mandiant found was a Docker project, MC-Based-Stock-Invest-Simulator-main, sending traffic to getstockprice[.]com, a domain registered through Namecheap on February 2. Its files sat in the developer's Downloads folder, which Sygnia, relaying Mandiant, reads as suggesting a potential social engineering vector.

How the developer came to run it is not published. The Hacker News notes earlier cases in which TraderTraitor approached exchange developers on Telegram about a Docker project, and says it is not clear the same method was used here. SecurityWeek reports, citing Mandiant, that the attackers posed as a trusted open-source contributor; other accounts say only likely social engineering. Safe says the attacker later removed the malware and cleared Bash history, so the payload was not recovered.

Palo Alto Networks Unit 42, which does not name Safe, lists the domain among the C2 infrastructure of the group it calls Slow Pisces. The page suggests watching for developer machines with production access contacting newly registered domains.

== A session MFA had already approved
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1528 | Steal Application Access Token
fact: Constraint | MFA again every 12 hours for STS sessions (Sygnia, CryptoSlate)
fact: Taken | an active AWS session token, "likely" by the malware (Sygnia)
fact: How issued | not published; Elastic suspects GetSessionToken or AWS SSO
fact: Bearer credential | whoever holds it makes the same API calls until expiry
MFA is checked when a session is issued, and a session that lives for hours on a compromised machine carries that check with it.
notes:
Safe described the attack as the compromise of the developer's laptop and "the hijacking of AWS session tokens to bypass multi-factor authentication," per The Hacker News.

Sygnia explains the constraint. Safe's configuration required MFA re-authentication for Security Token Service sessions every 12 hours, and the attackers had failed to register an MFA device of their own. So they relied on a hijacked, active session token, "likely extracted by the malware" on the workstation.

A temporary AWS session is a bearer credential: whoever holds the access key, secret key and session token can make the same API calls until it expires.

How the developer obtained it, and where it was stored, are not published. Elastic Security Labs, which rebuilt the attack in an emulation, suspects either the GetSessionToken API or an AWS SSO login through the CLI, both of which cache short-lived credentials locally. If the token was read from a file such as ~/.aws/credentials, the page says the matching technique is T1552.001, Credentials In Files, and Elastic's rule "Cloud Credential Files Accessed by Process in Suspicious Directory" looks for that read.

The page names two gaps: a 12-hour session, and, on the investigators' reading, a developer's access that reached the production web bucket. No published log names who wrote the files.

== Into AWS, on the developer's working hours
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Lateral Movement
technique: T1550.001 | Use Alternate Authentication Material: Application Access Token
technique: T1098.005 | Account Manipulation: Device Registration
fact: First access | February 5, 08:36:51 UTC, from ExpressVPN IP addresses
fact: Pattern | Kali user agent; kept to the developer's working hours
fact: Own MFA device | attempted 14:06:25 UTC, February 5; it failed
The clearest early signal: an MFA device enrollment from a temporary session.
notes:
Safe's timeline puts the first access to its AWS environment at 08:36:51 UTC on February 5, just under 24 hours after the workstation was compromised. Safe said the use of Developer1's AWS account "originated from ExpressVPN IP addresses," with user-agent strings containing distrib#kali.2024, which indicates Kali Linux, per The Hacker News. The page also maps the session's use to T1078.004, Cloud Accounts, and the VPN to T1090.002, External Proxy. Sygnia and Safe both say the attackers kept to the developer's working hours.

At 14:06:25 UTC the same day the attackers tried to register their own MFA device on the developer's identity, and failed. Safe has not said why. Elastic's overview suggests the session lacked MFA context, but its own emulation, using a session that had MFA context, also failed, and Elastic calls the reason unclear. Had it worked, the attackers could have started fresh MFA-backed sessions once the stolen one expired.

The page calls the attempt the clearest early signal in this chain: an MFA device enrollment from a temporary session, from a commercial VPN address, with an offensive-distribution user agent. Elastic publishes rules for it: "STS Temporary IAM Session Token Used from Multiple Addresses", "IAM Attempt to Register Virtual MFA Device with Temporary Credentials" and "AWS CLI with Kali Linux Fingerprint Identified".

== Twelve days in AWS, mostly undisclosed
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Discovery
technique: T1580 | Cloud Infrastructure Discovery
technique: T1619 | Cloud Storage Object Discovery
fact: Window | February 5 to 17, 2025
fact: Published detail | "reconnaissance", and undescribed C2 activity on February 17
fact: Likely, per Elastic | IAM and S3 enumeration; its hardening section says no IAM enumeration
fact: Found afterwards | AWS and workstation logs: 3 more UNC4899 domains (CryptoSlate)
The page maps discovery as likely, not shown.
notes:
Safe's timeline covers February 5 to 17 with one line, attacker reconnaissance in the AWS environment, and Sygnia notes that apart from the failed MFA registration not much has been disclosed.

At 03:22:44 UTC on February 17 the timeline records command and control activity in the AWS environment without saying what it was. Elastic reads it as the shift from reconnaissance to staging the attack, and separately notes a possible indication of persistence in AWS. The page leaves that unmapped.

Elastic's timeline says the attackers likely enumerated IAM roles and S3 buckets, and its emulation lists buckets with the AWS CLI. Its own hardening section then says the adversary did no IAM enumeration and went straight to S3. Elastic also assumes the attackers knew of the bucket because it served app.safe.global, whose files anyone could download. So the page maps cloud infrastructure and storage discovery as likely, not shown.

Mandiant's three additional UNC4899 domains turned up in Safe's AWS network logs, per CryptoSlate, so some network telemetry existed and was searched afterwards.

The page suggests watching for list and describe calls from a human session at volumes unlike its history, and any access to the production web bucket by an identity that does not deploy.

== Contracts deployed, and an apparent rehearsal
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Resource Development
technique: T1608 | Stage Capabilities
fact: Deployed | two attacker contracts, February 18, 2025 (CertiK)
fact: Slot-0 writer | 0x96221423...7242 imitates an ERC-20 transfer() (NCC)
fact: Fund mover | 0xbDd077f6...9516, backdoor transfer functions (CertiK)
fact: Apparent rehearsal | 9 delegatecalls from a second Safe (this page's reading)
This page infers a rehearsal; no published source says so.
notes:
CertiK records two attacker contracts deployed on February 18, 2025, three days before the theft. NCC Group describes the first: it imitates an ERC-20 transfer() but writes its first argument to storage slot 0, which in a Safe proxy holds the address of the implementation contract, the masterCopy. The second contains backdoor functions to transfer funds, per CertiK. Run through a delegatecall from Bybit's wallet, the first would execute in the wallet's own storage and point the wallet at the second.

The tampered JavaScript at stage 06 lists a second Safe beside Bybit's, which Sygnia calls "likely associated with the attackers." Safe's public Transaction Service, read for the page, shows a 3-of-6 Safe running the same v1.1.1 implementation as Bybit's wallet. It executed nine delegatecalls into the slot-0 writer between 19:16 UTC on February 18 and 12:32 UTC on February 20, four of them submitted by the address that later submitted the theft.

The page infers it was the attackers' rehearsal wallet, and says no published source says so. Keep that label.

For detection the page offers threat intelligence only: the contracts were public on chain for three days before the theft.

== JavaScript swapped in Safe's S3 bucket
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Supply Chain
technique: T1195.002 | Supply Chain Compromise: Compromise Software Supply Chain
fact: Written | about 15:29 UTC, February 19; at least two files, per Sygnia
fact: Origin | S3 behind CloudFront; file name, build hash included, unchanged
fact: In the browser | no SRI; CSP allowed 'self' (this page's reading)
fact: Logged | no published AWS log of the write; no identity named
Nothing is reported to have checked the files in the bucket. The attack skipped source code and peer review and wrote built files straight in.
notes:
The main file was _next/static/chunks/pages/_app-52c9031bfa03da47.js, the main Next.js bundle; Sygnia shows at least one other. The page also maps the change to T1565.001, Stored Data Manipulation. Sygnia and Verichains both found JavaScript modified "directly on the S3 bucket" serving app.safe.global. Verichains concluded that an AWS S3 or CloudFront account or API key of Safe's "was likely leaked or compromised," per BleepingComputer. Sygnia found the tampered bundle in the Chrome caches of all three signers' machines.

Safe has described controls on the path from source code to production: few people with infrastructure access, source code kept apart from infrastructure management, and peer review before production changes, per CryptoSlate. The attack went around all of it, with access Safe says came from one of the very few staff who held it. No published account describes any control at Safe noticing the changed files in the roughly 47 hours the malicious code was served, and none says whether Safe logged S3 object-level writes.

By the page's reading of the archived page from February 20, its scripts had no integrity attributes, and its Content Security Policy allowed scripts from its own origin, where the tampered files came from. The browser had no reason to refuse them. Safe added SRI hashes in March 2025, in pull requests #5190 and #5199.

== What CloudTrail does not log by default
layout: background
source: AWS CloudTrail User Guide, logging data events | https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-data-events-with-cloudtrail.html
AWS's CloudTrail guide: data events record operations performed on or in a resource, such as S3 PutObject, GetObject and DeleteObject. They are often high-volume. By default, trails and event data stores do not log them, and they cost extra.
notes:
Background, from the AWS CloudTrail User Guide, in general terms.

AWS says data events "provide information about the resource operations performed on or in a resource," also known as data plane operations, and that they are often high-volume activities. Its examples include Amazon S3 object-level API activity, such as the GetObject, DeleteObject and PutObject operations on objects in S3 buckets.

The guide is explicit about the default: "By default, trails and event data stores do not log data events." Additional charges apply for them.

Logging data events need not mean logging everything. AWS says advanced event selectors can create fine-grained selectors, "which help you control costs by only logging the specific events of interest," and trails can log data events for S3 objects with them.

This slide explains the category only. It says nothing about what Safe logged, which no published source states.

== The injected code fired for two Safes only
layout: stage
stage: 07
phase: Stealth
technique: T1480 | Execution Guardrails
technique: T1565.003 | Data Manipulation: Runtime Data Manipulation
fact: Targets | Bybit's cold wallet 0x1db92e2e...dfcf4, and 0x19c6876e...d141
fact: Condition | connected Safe in the list, and operation 0, a plain call
fact: After signing | original fields restored for display and for Safe's servers
fact: Blind-signed (NCC) | all three; Zhou says he did not check his Ledger (CertiK)
The signers checked the web interface, which was running the attacker's code.
notes:
NCC Group published the injected code, which the page also maps to T1059.007, JavaScript. It checks the connected Safe against two addresses, Bybit's cold wallet and the second Safe from stage 05. If one matches and the transaction is a plain call, operation 0, it rewrites the transaction, as the next slide shows. After the wallet signs, the code restores the original fields "immediately after the signature and before the data was proposed to the server," as NCC puts it, so the Safe interface and the other signers see the transfer they expected.

By the page's reading of the archived file, the same swap also wraps the code path that executes a transaction. A second list of two addresses makes the page reload when one is the connected signer; by the page's reading, one is an owner of the rehearsal Safe and the other is one of Bybit's own signers, who did not sign the theft. No source explains the list. Sygnia also describes tampering with a transaction validation function.

Bybit's CEO, Ben Zhou, said the interface showed the correct address and data, but the data sent to the Ledger was altered, and he did not verify it on the Ledger screen, per CertiK. NCC writes that blind signing should be expected in any threat model.

== What the signers saw, and what their wallets signed
layout: compare
col: Shown in the Safe interface | An apparently routine internal transfer (NCC) | A plain call, operation 0 | Correct address and data, Zhou says (CertiK) | Put back once the wallet signed
col: Swapped in for signing | to: the slot-0 writer, 0x9622...7242 | operation 1, a delegatecall | value 0; safeTxGas 45746 | data: transfer(0xbdd0...9516, 0)
NCC: the data is not human-readable, which makes transactions easy to confuse.
notes:
This slide sets the two versions of one transaction side by side, from NCC Group's reading of the injected code.

On the left is what the signers saw. NCC describes the transaction as an apparently routine internal transfer, and the code acted only when it was a plain call, operation 0. Bybit's CEO, Ben Zhou, said the Safe interface showed the correct address and data, per CertiK. Once the wallet had signed, the code put the original fields back, so the interface and the other signers saw the transfer they expected.

On the right is what the code swapped in. It pointed the transaction at the slot-0 writer contract, set the operation to 1, a delegatecall, the value to 0 and safeTxGas to 45746, and replaced the data with a transfer() naming the fund-moving contract.

NCC describes the January 14 mETH transfer as an earlier intended delegatecall that could make the malicious one look familiar. By the page's reading, Safe's Transaction Service records it as a plain call, and the theft as the only delegatecall among the wallet's 72 executed transactions. What set the malicious one apart was the operation type and an unfamiliar destination contract.

== The wallet replaced, then swept
layout: stage
stage: 08
phase: Impact
technique: T1556 | Modify Authentication Process
technique: T1657 | Financial Theft
fact: Transaction | 0x46deef0f...7882, block 21,895,238, 14:13:35 UTC, February 21
fact: Sent by | an attacker address, not a Bybit signer (NCC)
fact: Effect | storage slot 0, the masterCopy, set to the fund-moving contract
fact: Swept | 14:16:11 UTC, 401,346.77 ETH and more (this page's reading)
156 seconds to the sweep: the controls that count mostly act before signing.
notes:
The transaction carrying the three signatures was sent by an attacker address, not by a Bybit signer; NCC notes it was "sent directly by the attacker." NCC also notes that signatures sent to Safe's Client Gateway can be read through Safe's APIs, though none of the sources sets out exactly how the attackers gathered these three.

Run as a delegatecall, the attacker contract's transfer() wrote the fund-moving contract's address into storage slot 0, so Bybit's wallet now ran the attacker's code and its three-signature threshold no longer stood in the way, per NCC and CertiK. ATT&CK has no technique for replacing a smart contract wallet's implementation, so the page maps the effect, the signature check removed, to T1556 as the nearest fit.

By the page's reading of the chain, a test sweep moved 90 USDT at 14:15:11 UTC, and four sweeps moved 401,346.77 ETH, 90,375.55 stETH, 15,000 cmETH and 8,000 mETH at 14:16:11; NCC rounds the ETH to 401,347. The FBI puts the theft at about $1.5 billion; Elliptic counts about $1.46 billion. One part came back: the 15,000 cmETH had to wait out mETH Protocol's withdrawal delay, eight hours here, in which mETH Protocol blocked it, and Bybit says mETH Protocol retrieved the tokens. The laundering that followed is outside the page's scope.

== How one delegatecall replaced the wallet
layout: layers
layer: Bybit's wallet | A Safe proxy: storage slot 0 holds the masterCopy | Safe's API labels it version 1.1.1 (NCC)
layer: Delegatecall | The slot-0 writer's transfer(), run in the wallet's storage
layer: Slot 0 rewritten | The masterCopy now points at the fund mover, 0xbdd0...9516
layer: The sweep | The attacker's code runs; the threshold no longer applies
From NCC and CertiK, as the page reports them.
notes:
This slide draws only the mechanism the page describes, from the top layer down.

Bybit's cold wallet was a Safe proxy. In a Safe proxy, storage slot 0 holds the address of the implementation contract, the masterCopy. NCC identifies the wallet's implementation as a Gnosis Safe masterCopy that Safe's API labels version 1.1.1.

The signed transaction was a delegatecall into the slot-0 writer from stage 05, so that contract's code ran in the wallet's own storage. Its transfer() imitates an ERC-20 transfer but writes its first argument to storage slot 0.

That argument was the fund-moving contract, so the masterCopy now pointed at it. From then on, per NCC and CertiK, Bybit's wallet ran the attacker's code, and its three-signature threshold no longer stood in the way. The sweep followed.

Nothing on chain stopped the swap. Safe's Guardrail guard, published in August 2025, allows delegatecalls only to allowlisted contracts and is designed for Safe v1.5.0 and above. NCC goes further: a wallet built only for the transfers Bybit needed would have had no built-in delegatecall to arbitrary contracts for an attacker to use.

== Restored about two minutes after the theft
layout: stage
stage: 09
phase: Stealth
technique: T1070 | Indicator Removal
technique: T1070.003 | Indicator Removal: Clear Command History
fact: Live | about 2 days before the theft; about 2 minutes after it
fact: Who | Sygnia: likely the attackers, "presumably to cover their track"
fact: On the Mac | malware removed, Bash history cleared (Safe)
fact: Survived | the signers' Chrome caches; Internet Archive captures
The two days were before the theft; the restore came before the main sweep.
notes:
Sygnia determined that the tampered resources were modified again at 14:15 UTC on February 21, "approximately two minutes after the malicious transaction was executed," and that the attackers likely did it, "presumably to cover their track." No published source names the identity that wrote the files back.

So the malicious code was served for about two days before the theft, from 15:29 UTC on February 19, and for about two minutes after it. The Hacker News's phrase, that the code was injected "for a two-day period between February 19 and 21," describes the same window. Do not let anyone read it as two days after the theft.

On the developer's Mac, Safe said, the attacker "removed their malware and cleared Bash history in an effort to thwart investigative efforts." The page maps these to T1070.004, File Deletion, and T1070.003, Clear Command History.

The cleanup did not erase the evidence. The tampered bundle survived in the Chrome caches on the signers' machines and in the Internet Archive, and those copies, not a log at Safe, are what Sygnia and NCC worked from.

The page suggests watching for a second unplanned write to the same object minutes after a large outbound transfer. With versioning on, the bucket keeps both versions.

== February 21 in UTC: the theft, the restore, the sweep
layout: timeline
event: 14:13:35 | The disguised delegatecall executes
event: 14:15:11 | Test sweep, 90 USDT (this page's reading)
event: 14:15:13 | Safe's restore time; the second file's Last-Modified (Sygnia)
event: 14:15:32 | Main bundle restored, by its Last-Modified (this page's reading)
event: 14:16:11 | Four sweeps empty the wallet (this page's reading)
event: February 24 | Sygnia finds the malicious code gone
Sygnia: modified again at about 14:15. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
Every time on this slide is one the page gives, in UTC on February 21 unless marked. The markers are evenly spaced, and the gaps between them are not to scale.

The theft transaction executed at 14:13:35. By the page's reading of the chain, a test sweep moved 90 USDT at 14:15:11.

Sygnia puts the restore at about 14:15. Safe's timeline gives 14:15:13 and labels it a Wayback Machine snapshot, but the archive holds no capture at that time, and 14:15:13 is the Last-Modified time Sygnia shows for the second file. The archive's next copy of the main bundle gives the restored object a Last-Modified time of 14:15:32, 117 seconds after the theft transaction, with the same ETag as the February 13 build. Those readings are the page's own. By those timestamps the restore came 98 to 117 seconds after the theft.

The restore came before the 14:16:11 sweep. Sygnia's own check of app.safe.global on February 24 found the malicious code gone.

== Two defenses against stages 01 to 05
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05
row: Make a stolen developer session worth less | 01 | 02 | 03
row: Responding: investigate the cloud account, not only the laptop | 01 | 04
No defense on the page names stage 05. The response defense also names stage 09.
notes:
The page gives nine defenses. Two of them reach the first five stages, and a cell is marked only where the defense's own words name something from that stage.

Making a stolen developer session worth less names the Docker project run from the Downloads folder of the machine that held the session, stage 01; Safe's sessions of up to 12 hours between MFA prompts, stage 02; and the MFA persistence attempt in step 03, which Elastic says an IAM condition would have prevented.

The response defense names the laptop and Safe's new developer machines, stage 01, and the investigation of activity in Safe's AWS account, stage 04, where Safe has published little. It also names the signers' browser caches and the web archive, which belong to stage 09, on the next slide.

Now look at the empty column. No defense on the page names stage 05: the two contracts deployed on chain three days before the theft, and what the page reads as a rehearsal on a second Safe. That gap is the second discussion question.

== Eight defenses against stages 06 to 09
layout: matrix
columns: 06 | 07 | 08 | 09
row: Only the deploy pipeline writes the bucket | 06
row: Log every write; alert outside the pipeline | 06 | 09
row: Compare what the CDN serves with the build | 06
row: Versioning, and Object Lock's limit | 06 | 09
row: Subresource Integrity, and its limit | 06
row: Verify signing without the web interface | 07
row: Refuse unneeded delegatecalls | 07 | 08
row: Responding: investigate the cloud account | 09
Five of the nine defenses meet stage 06, the cloud lesson.
notes:
These eight defenses cover the second half of the chain.

Five meet stage 06. Letting only the pipeline write names the overwritten frontend files. Logging every write names the S3 writes and Safe's labels for both changes, the swap and the restore at stage 09. Comparing with the build names the unchanged file name. Versioning names the replaced object and the restored copy at stage 09. Subresource Integrity names the missing integrity attributes and the CSP that allowed the tampered files.

Verifying what a signer signs names the blind signing and the Ledger at stage 07. Refusing unneeded delegatecalls names the theft's delegatecall, the wallet's first by the page's reading, at stage 07, and the v1.1.1 wallet that Guardrail does not cover, at stage 08.

The response defense names the signers' browser caches and the public web archive, the evidence that survived stage 09.

The next four slides take the defenses in groups.

== Restrict and log every write to the bucket
layout: compare
col: One deploy role writes the bucket | Elastic: developers "should never have direct write access" | Deny s3:PutObject and s3:DeleteObject to all but that role | Only CI's OIDC token for production can assume it | Protect those policies and CloudFront's origin too
col: Log and alert on every write | CloudTrail write data events: off by default, billed separately | Alert on any write the deploy role did not make | Elastic's rule, as published, matches only static/js/; Safe's path was _next/static/chunks/
The policies are this page's reading of Elastic's advice.
notes:
These two defenses are the cloud lesson.

Let only the deploy pipeline write the production web bucket. On the investigators' reading, credentials from one developer's session could overwrite production frontend files, and Safe's peer reviews never saw the change because it never passed through source code. Elastic recommends that developers "should never have direct write access to production S3 buckets," with uploads only from OIDC-authenticated CI. As the page reads that advice: a bucket policy denying s3:PutObject and s3:DeleteObject to every principal except one deploy role, and a trust policy on that role accepting only the CI provider's OIDC token for the production branch or environment, so no human session can assume it. Restrict who can change those policies and the CloudFront origin, for example with a service control policy, or an identity that can edit them can undo the deny. Keep an alarmed break-glass path.

Log every write to that bucket. CloudTrail records PutObject only as a data event, and by default trails do not log data events. Turn on write data events and alert on any write the deploy role did not make. Elastic's rule "S3 Static Site JavaScript File Uploaded via PutObject" matches only static/js/ as published; a Next.js site has to widen it. An alert on any non-deploy write has no such gap.

== Check the served files, keep the old ones, pin the scripts
layout: compare
col: Compare with the build | Fetch every script through the CDN on a schedule | Compare each hash with the build's manifest | Would have shown the change on February 19 | This page's suggestion
col: Versioning, Object Lock | Versioning keeps the replaced object | AWS: Object Lock settings "don't prevent new versions" | It keeps the original; it does not stop the swap
col: SRI, and its limit | February: no SRI (this page's reading) | NCC: works if attackers cannot change the HTML | If HTML shares the bucket, one writer can usually change both (this page's reasoning)
notes:
Three defenses for the files themselves.

Compare what the CDN serves with what the build produced. The tampered bundle kept its name, so nothing about the URL changed. A scheduled job that fetches every script and page through the CDN and compares each hash with the build's manifest would have shown the change on February 19, two days before the theft. This is the page's suggestion, not a source's; an outside archive caught both versions. Elastic suggests versioning, with monitoring for overwrites of critical JavaScript.

Turn on versioning, and know what Object Lock does not do. Elastic recommends Object Lock to prevent overwriting, but AWS says retention periods and legal holds "don't prevent new versions of the object from being created," and an upload with the same key becomes the version served. Object Lock keeps the original safe for recovery. The write restriction, with its policies protected, is what stops the swap.

Add Subresource Integrity, and know its limit. NCC says pinning would have prevented the attack if the attackers could change the JavaScript but not the HTML. The archived HTML page was itself served from S3 through CloudFront. When it comes from the same bucket, an identity that can write one can usually write both. That last point is the page's reasoning.

== Shorten the session, and investigate the cloud account
layout: compare
col: Make a stolen session worth less | Safe's sessions: up to 12 hours between MFA prompts | Elastic: tokens of an hour or less; Identity Center for humans | Elastic: deny iam:CreateVirtualMFADevice to temporary credentials | High-value targets: engineers who can write production
col: Investigate the cloud account too | Safe says it rotated credentials, provisioned new machines (SecurityWeek) | Sygnia: the largest gap, a comprehensive investigation of Safe's AWS account | Left-behind persistence would let attackers return | Preserve browser caches and web archives early
notes:
Two defenses for identity and response.

Make a stolen developer session worth less. Safe's sessions ran up to 12 hours between MFA prompts, and the attackers worked inside them for days. Elastic recommends session tokens of an hour or less, IAM Identity Center for all human users, and removing sts:GetSessionToken from IAM users. It also recommends IAM conditions that deny sensitive calls such as iam:CreateVirtualMFADevice when made with temporary credentials, which Elastic says would have prevented the MFA persistence attempt at stage 03. Engineers who can write production are high-value targets: the machine that held this session also ran a Docker project from its Downloads folder.

If you are responding, investigate the cloud account, not only the laptop. Safe says it rotated all credentials, reset clusters, rotated keys and secrets, provisioned new developer machines and redeployed container images, per SecurityWeek. Sygnia's view in March was that the most significant remaining gap was a comprehensive investigation of activity in Safe's AWS account, because persistence left behind would let the attackers come back. In this chain the decisive evidence came from outside the victim, in the signers' browser caches and a public web archive, so preserve both early.

== Verify what the signer signs, and refuse unneeded delegatecalls
layout: compare
col: Verify without the web interface | CertiK: a second device, simulation, command-line tools | NCC: wallets that display EIP-712; plan for blind signing | NCC: EIP-712 cannot decode nested operations like this | This page: recompute the Safe transaction hash on a separate machine
col: Refuse unneeded delegatecalls | The theft's delegatecall was the wallet's first (this page's reading) | Guardrail: allowlist and delay, Safe v1.5.0 and above; Bybit's ran v1.1.1 | Safe calls Guardrail an introductory example: audit first | NCC: a purpose-built wallet has no arbitrary delegatecall
notes:
Two defenses at the wallet.

Verify what a signer signs without trusting the web interface. Bybit's signers checked the Safe interface, which was running the attacker's code, and blind-signed, per NCC; Zhou says he did not check the data on his Ledger, per CertiK. CertiK recommends a second device to verify transaction data before signing, simulating transactions, and command-line tools over third-party web interfaces. NCC recommends hardware wallets that display EIP-712 messages, and states the limit: EIP-712 cannot decode nested operations like this one, so such a device would have shown a delegatecall to an unfamiliar contract but not what the call did. By the page's reading, all three theft signatures were eth_sign signatures over the transaction hash, so a signer who recomputes the hash on a separate machine from the intended fields sees the mismatch.

Refuse delegatecalls the wallet does not need. Safe's Guardrail guard allows delegatecalls only to allowlisted contracts, with a delay before a new one can be added, and targets Safe v1.5.0 and above. Safe presents it as an introductory example, not for a large treasury without a professional audit. NCC confirmed that Safe's Client Gateway now rejects a proposal without a matching hash and signature, though attackers who control the web app's JavaScript can still change what signers see.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Can anything besides your deploy pipeline write the bucket serving your web app?
check: Are that bucket's writes logged, with alerts on any the pipeline did not make?
check: Does anything compare what your CDN serves with what the build produced?
check: Is the bucket versioned, and do you know what Object Lock does not stop?
check: How long do human AWS sessions last between MFA prompts?
check: Do signers verify the transaction hash on a separate machine before signing?
check: Does your wallet refuse delegatecalls to contracts outside an audited allowlist?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether anything other than your deploy pipeline can write the bucket your web app is served from, and who can change the bucket policy, the deploy role's trust policy and the CDN's origin. Ask whether CloudTrail write data events are on for that bucket, and whether a write the deploy role did not make would alert anyone. If you use a published rule, check that its path matches yours.

Ask whether anything fetches what your CDN serves and compares it with the build's hashes. Ask whether the bucket is versioned, and remember that Object Lock keeps the original but does not stop a new version being served.

Ask how long a human AWS session lasts between MFA prompts, and whether temporary credentials can register an MFA device.

Finally, ask whether your signers recompute the transaction hash on a separate machine before signing, and whether your wallet refuses delegatecalls to contracts outside an audited allowlist. If you were responding to this, ask whether you would investigate the cloud account as well as the laptop.

== Who would notice a changed file in your web bucket?
layout: question
For roughly 47 hours, Safe's bucket served a tampered bundle under its original name; no published account says anything at Safe noticed. Pick a bucket behind one of your sites. What would notice a write, and who would hear?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one.

The page's facts: at about 15:29 UTC on February 19, the attackers overwrote built JavaScript in Safe's S3 bucket and kept the file name, build hash included. No published account describes any control at Safe noticing the changed files in the roughly 47 hours the malicious code was served. The investigators reconstructed the change afterwards from the signers' Chrome caches and public web archives, and no published source shows the uploads in an AWS log or names the identity that made them.

Safe's controls guarded the path from source code to production, with peer review before production changes. The attack never passed through source code.

Ask each person to pick one bucket that serves a site in their own environment and say what would notice a write to it, and who would hear about it, before you show the answer slide.

== Lock the write path, log every write, compare with the build
layout: answer
From stage 06:
- Uploads only from OIDC-authenticated CI (Elastic), through one deploy role (this page's reading).
- Write data events on, alerting on any write outside the deploy role.
- Served files compared with the build's hashes (this page's suggestion).
notes:
The page's answer has three parts, and each closes a different gap.

First, the write path. Elastic says developers should never have direct write access to production S3 buckets, with uploads only from OIDC-authenticated CI. The page reads that as a bucket policy that denies writes to every principal but one deploy role, a trust policy that lets only the CI provider's OIDC token assume it, and protection for those policies and the CDN origin.

Second, the log. CloudTrail does not log data events by default, so S3 object writes go unrecorded unless you turn them on. Alert on any write the deploy role did not make, which has none of the path gap in Elastic's published rule.

Third, the comparison. The file name never changed, so only a check of the served bytes against the build would have shown the swap on February 19. That control is the page's suggestion, not a source's.

Bring the group's answers back to these. Object Lock and Subresource Integrity help, within the limits the page sets out.

== What would you add for stage 05?
layout: question
The page ties no defense to stage 05, where the attackers deployed two contracts on February 18 and, by this page's reading, rehearsed the swap on a second Safe. What would you add, and which reported detail would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from the empty column in the first defense matrix.

At stage 05, per CertiK, the attackers deployed two contracts on February 18, three days before the theft. NCC describes the first as imitating an ERC-20 transfer() while writing its first argument to storage slot 0. The second carried backdoor functions to transfer funds.

By the page's reading of Safe's Transaction Service, a second Safe, a 3-of-6 Safe on the same v1.1.1 implementation as Bybit's, ran nine delegatecalls into the first contract between February 18 and 20. The page infers it was a rehearsal wallet and says no published source says so.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught.

== The page ties no defense to stage 05
layout: answer
This deck adds none, so any control you propose is your own. Test it against the page's details: contracts public on chain for three days before the theft, and its detect line, threat intelligence only.
notes:
Be clear that the page stops here. It ties no defense to stage 05, and this deck adds none of its own.

The page's detection line for stage 05 says threat intelligence only: the contracts were public on chain for three days before the theft.

The group may argue that refusing delegatecalls to contracts outside an allowlist would have made the contracts useless, whatever happened at stage 05. The page ties that defense to the signing and the theft at stages 07 and 08, not to the deployment, so that argument is the group's own.

Keep the rehearsal labelled when the group discusses it. The second Safe is in the injected code's target list, and Sygnia calls it likely associated with the attackers, but the rehearsal is the page's inference from the chain.

== Primary sources
layout: links
link: FBI, North Korea responsible (I-022625-PSA) | https://www.ic3.gov/PSA/2025/PSA250226
link: Sygnia, Bybit: what we know so far | https://www.sygnia.co/blog/sygnia-investigation-bybit-hack/
link: NCC Group, technical analysis | https://www.nccgroup.com/research/in-depth-technical-analysis-of-the-bybit-hack/
link: Elastic Security Labs, Bit ByBit emulation | https://www.elastic.co/security-labs/bit-bybit
link: Safe Ecosystem Foundation, Safe{Wallet}'s February 26 statement | https://safefoundation.org/blog/safe-ecosystem-foundation-statement
link: CryptoSlate, Safe's internal investigation | https://cryptoslate.com/safes-internal-investigation-reveals-developers-laptop-breach-led-to-bybit-hack/
link: The Hacker News, Safe{Wallet} confirms TraderTraitor | https://thehackernews.com/2025/03/safewallet-confirms-north-korean.html
link: CertiK, Bybit incident technical analysis | https://www.certik.com/resources/blog/bybit-incident-technical-analysis
notes:
These are primary sources the csoh.org kill chain cites. Every fact about the incident in this deck comes from that page, which draws on these.

The FBI's public service announcement carries the attribution and the $1.5 billion figure. Sygnia's investigation and NCC Group's analysis carry the forensic findings and the injected code. Elastic Security Labs rebuilt the attack and supplies most of the cloud recommendations. The Safe Ecosystem Foundation page carries Safe's February 26 statement, and CryptoSlate and The Hacker News report Safe's March update and its quotes. CertiK covers the contracts and Zhou's account of signing.

The page also cites Cointelegraph for Safe's timeline, SecurityWeek, BleepingComputer, crypto.news, Bybit's own statements, ChainCatcher, Unit 42, Elliptic, the Safe Foundation's Guardrail post, Internet Archive captures, Safe's two SRI pull requests, Blockscout, Safe's Transaction Service, three AWS documentation pages, and MITRE ATT&CK pages for four techniques. They are left off this slide for space.

The background slide names its own source, the AWS CloudTrail User Guide.

